Hi,

in the latest CVS version, there are a few new topics regarding the
better surround experience.  I'd like to summarize below.

* Upmix plugin

It's a plug to upmix to 4 or 6-channel stream.
Almost the same functionality can be achieved via a route plugin with
a proper ttable, but this one is pretty easier to use.  The upmix
plugin accpept inputs from 1 to 6 channels, and reroutes/processes
automagically.

The setup is like:

	pcm.upmix51 {
		type plug
		slave.pcm {
			type upmix
			slave.pcm "surround51"
		}
	}

and you can use it:

	% aplay -Dupmix51 some-2ch.wav

See alsa-plugins/doc/upmix.txt for details.

Using this together with dmix is doable but still complicated.  I'll
try to implement easier setup in the next ALSA version.


* Vdownmix plugin

It's a plugin to downmix from 4 or 6 channels to 2 channels, with a
bit better sourrund effect.  It's good only for headphone output, not
for speaker.

See alsa-plugins/doc/vdownmix.txt for example settings.


* DPL and DPL-II

The encoding from 4-6 channel surround to 2-channel stereo for DPL and
DPL-II can be done via the PCM definition in pcm/dpl.conf.

For example, add the following to ~/.asoundrc:

	<confdir:pcm/dpl.conf>
	pcm.updpl {
		type plug
		slave.pcm {
			type upmix
			slave.pcm "dpl:default"
			channels 6
		}
	}

This will upmix inputs to 6-channels,  encode to 2-channel and then
output to the default PCM.  When you have a DPL-capable receiver, the
signal will be expanded to L, C, R and S.  So, it'd be nice for
showing a DVD, for example.

You can replace "surround51" or "surround40" with this PCM if you
want, by adding the following to ~/.asoundrc:

	pcm.!surround51 "updpl"
	pcm.!surround40 "updpl"

For DPL-II, use "dpl2" instead of "dpl" (the config file is identical)

	<confdir:pcm/dpl.conf>
	pcm.updpl2 {
		type plug
		slave.pcm {
			type upmix
			slave.pcm "dpl2:default"
			channels 6
		}
	}

I've not tested dpl2, so let me know whether it works or not, if you
have a receiver.  You can test with speaker-test:

	% speaker-test -c6 -Dupdpl2 -tw


